// The Cedar SCHEMA for the platform authorization vocabulary.
//
// WHAT IT IS FOR. Cedar's parser accepts any action id, any entity type and any
// context key, because a bare `PolicySet::from_str` has nothing to compare them
// against. `Action::"apps:raed"` parses, loads, and denies every request it was
// written to permit - with no diagnostic anywhere, and an audit row that reads
// exactly like an honest non-match. This file is the thing to compare against:
// `engine::load_platform_policies` validates the shipped bands against it under
// `ValidationMode::Strict` and REFUSES TO RETURN on any error or warning, and
// `eval::build_request` binds every request to it so a request whose action,
// principal type, resource type or context shape is outside this vocabulary is
// an error rather than a silent deny.
//
// A SCHEMA THAT ONLY EXISTED AT BOOT WOULD BE A NEW WAY TO FAIL. Every service
// that authorizes anything calls `load_platform_policies` on startup, so a
// malformed schema would take the fleet down at deploy time. `build.rs` parses
// this file and re-runs the same strict validation over every `.cedar` file
// under this directory, so both failures are compile errors on the developer's
// machine instead.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `appliesTo` IS THE REQUEST SURFACE, NOT THE POLICY PAIRING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every action lists every resource type on purpose, and the alternative was
// measured rather than assumed. A schema whose `appliesTo` was derived from the
// pairs the bands actually write would refuse most live requests at
// `Request::new`: `is_authorized_anywhere` probes `Resource::Any` first, then
// every organization the principal is seated in, then a representative project,
// for WHATEVER action it was asked about - so the consent screen legitimately
// asks "may this principal do `organization:members:write` at a Project?" and
// expects the answer "no". Under a narrow schema that question is not a denial;
// it is an `AuthzError::CedarRequest` raised before `audit_decision` runs, so
// it surfaces as a 500 with no durable record.
//
// WHAT THIS COSTS, stated plainly: the schema does NOT check that an
// organization-scoped action is paired with an `Organization` resource. Nothing
// here would notice a band permitting `organization:admin` at `resource is App`.
// That fence is the SCOPE of each band (`resource is Organization`, never a
// condition in the body) and it is bound by
// `crates/zeroship-authz/tests/platform_policies_test.rs`, which drives real
// principals at real ranks against real resource types.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ENTITY ATTRIBUTES
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `User` carries the two attributes `entities::user_entity` puts on it and
// nothing else. Authority - the two rank integers - deliberately does NOT ride
// here: a policy dereferencing a missing ENTITY attribute is SKIPPED with an
// evaluation error that `matched_policy_ids` never records, which is a deny
// indistinguishable from a clean no-match. The ranks are context keys, where a
// missing value denies at a comparison instead. See `crates/zeroship-authz/src/
// entities.rs`.
//
// `App`, `Project` and `Organization` carry no attributes because no policy
// dereferences one. `Resource` is the synthetic platform-level type whose only
// member is `Resource::"*"` (`Resource::Any`), the resource for surfaces that
// name nothing: create an organization, list the ones you belong to, read your
// own account.

entity User = {
  "email_verified": Bool,
  "account_locked": Bool,
};

entity App;
entity Project;
entity Organization;
entity Resource;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE REQUEST CONTEXT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `eval::build_context` supplies EVERY key below on EVERY request,
// unconditionally, and supplies nothing else. The authz test
// `every_action_builds_a_schema_bound_request_at_every_resource_type` checks
// this through the real request builder and schema. Missing or extra keys
// prevent the request from being constructed.
//
//   now             wall-clock seconds. Supplied truthfully; read by no policy
//                   and no `Condition` lowering today. It is kept - unlike the
//                   MFA keys, which were deleted in the same sweep - because it
//                   is a HONEST unused input rather than a falsified one: every
//                   producer of a `VerifiedPrincipal` hardcoded
//                   `mfa_verified: false`, so a condition reading it would have
//                   silently never matched, while `now` is the real clock.
//   now_minute_utc  minute of the UTC day, the input to `Condition::TimeWindow`.
//   request_ip      the input to `Condition::IpRange`. `0.0.0.0` when the edge
//                   did not supply one, so an IpRange condition fails closed.
//   effective_rank  organization rank, already narrowed to
//                   min(organization, project) for a Project- or App-scoped
//                   request unless the caller is admin or above. Zero for a
//                   principal with no live membership.
//   billing_rank    the SECOND authority axis, organization-level always: there
//                   is no per-project invoice to narrow against.
type RequestContext = {
  "now": Long,
  "now_minute_utc": Long,
  "request_ip": ipaddr,
  "effective_rank": Long,
  "billing_rank": Long,
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ACTION VOCABULARY
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every action in `zeroship_authz::Action::all()` must build a request against
// this schema. The authz request-builder tests exercise every action and
// resource type, including operator-only actions absent from the policy set.
// Strict policy validation alone cannot detect a missing declaration for an
// action that no policy mentions.

action "apps:read" appliesTo {
  principal: [User],
  resource: [App, Project, Organization, Resource],
  context: RequestContext
};

action "apps:write" appliesTo {
  principal: [User],
  resource: [App, Project, Organization, Resource],
  context: RequestContext
};

action "apps:deploy" appliesTo {
  principal: [User],
  resource: [App, Project, Organization, Resource],
  context: RequestContext
};

